v 0.4.0 (in progress)
---------------------
* Added
    * The caches of an electron-by-electron walker - the slater state and the powers of the e-n
      distances - now live between the moves of a sweep instead of being rebuilt by every one of
      them: wfn.caches builds both, wfn.value_ratio_1e takes a ratio out of them without touching
      them and wfn.accept_1e commits an accepted move into them. VMC keeps them for the whole
      walk, DMC rebuilds them per walker per step, which costs what a single move of the sweep
      used to. EBES DMC no longer evaluates the whole wave function for every electron it moves:
      of the two evaluations a move cost, the value is now a determinant ratio and only the drift
      velocity is left. Not for backflow, which spreads a single-electron move over every
      electron within its cutoff, nor for a geminal, which is not a slater determinant
    * The drift velocity an electron-by-electron DMC move reads is now the drift of the one
      electron it moves, not of the whole configuration: wfn.drift_velocity_1e takes it out of
      the same caches. The row of the inverse matrix the moved electron owns is the only one its
      own trace needs, and replacing a column divides that row by Q, so the far end of a proposal
      is read out of the state the near end left, without an update and without a Q anywhere -
      it cancels against the weight the determinant gains. Slater and cusp gained a
      single-electron pass over the basis for the orbital gradients and the jastrow the gradient
      of the terms one electron takes part in. A sweep is left with two evaluations of the whole
      drift, one at each end, where it used to pay one per electron on top of one per accepted
      move, and what stays quadratic in the electron count is the local energy at the end of it
    * Omega (e-e-e) three-electron backflow term: OMEGA TERM block reader/writer,
      analytical value/gradient/laplacian and their parameter derivatives,
      no-cusp and particle-symmetry constraints, and varmin/emin support
      (parameter mask, scale, constraint matrix, projector, parameter derivatives).
      Note that PyCasino constrains the K_lmn to be symmetric under index exchange,
      while CASINO up to at least v2.13.1313 imposes the opposite sign and so makes
      them antisymmetric; omega parameters are not interchangeable between the codes
      until that is resolved.
    * AGP/geminal wave function (``psi_s : geminal``): casino/geminal.py, GEMINAL block
      reader for parameters.casl, and the pfaffian design doc (Milestone 1); the
      Hartree-Fock default geminal reproduces the Slater determinant
    * OPT_GEMINAL: the geminal parameters (the c coefficients, the upper triangle of every g
      and the unpaired columns u) now take part in varmin and emin. The optimizability flags the
      reader already parsed reach the wave function, the parameter derivatives are taken by
      finite differences of the determinants over an orbital pool that is built once, and the
      geminal block is written back to parameters.N.casl at the end of every cycle
    * The Constraints section of the GEMINAL block is read. Every member of a tied group takes
      the value and the optimizability of the one member that carries an explicit flag, and only
      that reference is an independent parameter, the rest being restored from it after every
      parameter update and left out of what is written back. Until now the section was skipped,
      so any constrained casl was a different wave function in PyCasino than in Casino
    * The geminal orbital pool is cusp corrected, over its own orbital range rather than the
      determinant's, so an all-electron gaussian geminal no longer carries the uncorrected
      nuclear cusp
    * The derivatives of the geminal w.r.t its own parameters are analytic, where they were
      finite differences of the determinants over a pool built once. A parameter of g or of u
      changes the geminal matrix by a rank one term - a column of the up-spin orbital pool times
      a row of the down-spin one, or times a unit row for an unpaired column - so tr(M^-1 • dM/dp)
      is one element of a matrix product rather than a determinant of its own, and the product
      with the derivative of a coordinate, which is rank one as well, is one element of another.
      The sum over the geminals collapses into d(ln(psi))/dp · (what this geminal carries - what
      the sum carries) + its weight · the derivative of what it carries, whose first half
      vanishes identically for a single geminal. Where a rank one term does not appear - the
      laplacian of the matrix is a sum over every electron - M^-1 • that sum is folded once per
      geminal instead. This takes an emin or varmin cycle from 2·nparam determinant evaluations
      per configuration to one inverse and a handful of matrix products, nparam being of the
      order of half the square of the orbital pool
    * Hessian and tressian of the geminal, so that psi_s : geminal with backflow : T is no longer
      an input error: the local energy reads the hessian and the derivative of the local energy
      w.r.t the backflow parameters reads the tressian, and both now come from the geminal rather
      than from the determinant. An entry of the geminal matrix carries an up electron through
      its row and a down electron through its column, so it has second and third derivatives
      across two electrons at once, which a slater determinant has none of. What makes them
      cheap is that M^-1 • dM is of rank one whatever the coordinate is - a column of the inverse
      times a row of orbital gradients for an up electron, a column times a unit row for a down
      one - so every trace of a product of such factors collapses into the one matrix
      s[a, b] = row(a) • col(b). The gradient is its diagonal, the second trace is s[a,b]·s[b,a],
      and the two triple products of the third derivative are equal under a symmetric bb and
      contract into the diagonal of s • (s∘bb) • s. The second derivatives are rank one as well,
      which leaves the tressian a loop over pairs rather than over triples
* Fixes
    * VMC_METHOD 2 (DBDS) is refused by the input check. Nothing dispatches it, so a run that
      asked for it silently produced the initial configuration repeated VMC_NSTEP times
    * Under VMC_METHOD 4 the density of the asymmetric proposal leaked into the logarithm the
      walker carries, which nothing read, but which is the wave function of the walk
    * OPT_JASTROW, OPT_BACKFLOW, OPT_DET_COEFF and OPT_ORBITALS only ever reached the wave function
      through an opt_plan block, so a vmc_opt run that set them as plain keywords and relied on
      OPT_CYCLES optimized nothing at all. The keywords are now what every cycle an opt_plan block
      does not mention falls back to
    * The derivative of the local energy w.r.t. the determinant coefficients carried a spurious
      gradient**2 term (wfn.kinetic_energy_parameters_d1). The formula in wfn.rst is written in
      the d²lnPhi form and is right, but the laplacian it is fed comes in the d²Phi/Phi form,
      which already contains the square of the gradient, so the nablaPhi/Phi half of its bracket
      had to go. Affected every emin/varmin run with OPT_DET_COEFF since January 2026, and not by
      a little: on the four-determinant CASSCF(2,4) Be the spurious term is of order 1e3 where the
      derivative itself is of order 1. The new casino/tests/test_mdet.py compares both parameter
      derivatives of that wave function against finite differences
    * The Jastrow part of the emin gradient used the gradient of the Slater determinant even when
      the wave function was a geminal (wfn.kinetic_energy_parameters_d1)
    * psi_s : geminal with backflow : T is now an input error rather than a wave function whose
      local energy comes from the determinant while its drift comes from the geminal: the backflow
      branch of wfn.kinetic_energy needs a hessian, which the geminal does not have yet. The same
      check rejects ned > neu, which the neu x neu geminal matrix cannot describe
    * wfn.drift_kinetic_energy, the drift form F@F/2 of the kinetic energy, and its section in
      wfn.rst. Unbiased for the same expectation value as wfn.kinetic_energy by parts and differing
      only in variance, the pair being what Casino reports as FISQ and KEI. Neither dominates:
      Var(KEI)/Var(FISQ) measures 199 on He, 0.2 on Ne and 0.03 on pseudo-carbon. It is the
      estimator the VMC step size sum rule contains, and it was already computed inside
      kinetic_energy and merely not returned
    * slater.tressian_dot method returning third derivatives pre-contracted with the
      backflow Jacobian, and its documentation in slater.rst
    * Quick start guide and MPI parallelisation page in documentation
    * Documented all previously undocumented input keywords (GAUTOL, OPT_DTVMC, OPT_MAXITER,
      OPT_NOCTF_CYCLES, POSTFIT_VMC, OPT_FIXNL, VM_SMOOTH_LIMIT, VM_W_MAX, VM_W_MIN,
      EMIN_XI_VALUE, DMC_METHOD, LIMDMC, ALIMIT, NUCLEUS_GF_MODS, EBEST_AV_WINDOW, BACKFLOW)
    * drift_velocity and energy_parameters_d1 sections in wfn.rst
    * OPT_ORBITALS and USE_TMOVE keywords in config.rst
    * DMC tutorial page (dmc.rst): drift-diffusion, velocity limiting, branching,
      population control, T-move, load balancing
    * VMC tutorial page (vmc.rst): the time step derived from the kinetic-energy sum rule
      Var(ln(psi'^2/psi^2)) = 8/3 * dtvmc^2 * <T>, with the two averages (over the proposal
      at fixed R, and over the chain) kept apart, so that the classic ts*sqrt(<T>) = 0.826
      appears as what is obtained by interchanging them and the fitted correction as the
      Jensen gap of that interchange; the three kinetic-energy estimators KEI/TI/FISQ and
      where each of them enters; the Thomas-Fermi estimate of <T>; the range of validity;
      the EBES sqrt(N) law; acceptance_ratio and optimize_vmc_step
    * VMC_DECORR_PERIOD = 0 now selects the decorrelation period that maximizes efficiency
      instead of silently falling back to 3. The correlation time is measured on the production
      block itself, where thinning by the current period turns the autocorrelation rho into
      rho**period, so inverting that recovers the correlation time of the unthinned walk. A block
      of 1e6 configurations pins it down to better than +-0.5 even on Be, where a dedicated
      calibration run of the length CASINO uses leaves an uncertainty of +-20
    * Level-shift stabilization of the emin linear method: bisection over the shift, which only
      solves the eigenvalue problem, finds the largest step that still keeps every parameter
      variation within the trust radius, and the four
      shifts from there are compared by the correlated sampling estimate of energy + 3 * error
      over the very same configurations. That error accounts both for the effective sample size of
      the reweighting and for the serial correlation left after VMC_DECORR_PERIOD thinning, which
      reaches a factor of two on Be and N and would otherwise make the threshold system dependent.
      The no-step candidate always takes part in the comparison, so a cycle can never make the
      target function worse, and eigenvalues below EMIN_MIN_ENERGY are discarded as spurious.
      New keywords EMIN_MIN_ENERGY and EMIN_VAR_PREFACTOR
* Fixes
    * The cusp correction radius was fixed at 1/Z instead of being searched for. On C2H2 that put
      r_c of the carbon 3sigma_g orbital right next to a node of the s-part, where phi'(r_c)/phi(r_c)
      diverges, and the quartic fit blew up: VMC gave +374.46 instead of -76.85 au with a sample
      variance of 77953. The Casino search is now implemented: the largest radius below r_c,max at
      which the deviation from the ideal curve exceeds Z^2/CUSP_CONTROL, semi-optimized by +/-20%
      in nine steps, on a 0.0005 bohr grid with nodes located by bisection and blanked within
      0.02 bohr, r_c moved inwards out of a nodal region, and the shift set from the extremum of
      the s-part when a node stays inside r_c. New keyword CUSP_CONTROL
    * The effective one-electron local energy of the corrected orbital used an r-dependent Z_eff
      instead of the constant one defined by phi_tilde(0), and the ideal curve was scaled by Z^2
      rather than Z_eff^2, which matters for orbitals with a large Z_eff (-38 for the hydrogen
      1st orbital of C2H2)
    * cusp_info printed "positive" for every orbital because the sign was tested for truth rather
      than compared with zero, and reported Z_eff of the uncorrected rather than the corrected orbital
    * vmc_step_graph divided the acceptance ratio by the electron count unconditionally, so every
      CBCS graph it produced was scaled down by that factor
    * the EBES acceptance ratio was divided by the electron count, but a step is recorded as
      accepted when at least one electron moved, so the quantity was 1 - (1 - a)**N over N and
      could never exceed 1/N. For N = 7 it stayed within 0.8% of 1/N while the true acceptance
      swept 50% to 90%, leaving the step-size fit blind and unable to reach its 50% target.
      The per-electron probability is now recovered by inverting that expression, and the whole
      measurement lives in one place, Casino.acceptance_ratio, shared with vmc_step_graph
    * correlation.data writer emitted a single eta cut-off line whatever the spin-dependence,
      and lost the "2=YES BUT NO SPIN-DEP" flag because it is stored as a boolean, so the
      written file could not be read back
    * PSI_S header printed a hardcoded ``slater`` regardless of the actual psi_s value
    * Input keywords parsed with wrong type: VM_W_MAX, VM_W_MIN, EMIN_XI_VALUE read as
      booleans instead of floats, NON_LOCAL_GRID as boolean instead of integer;
      LIMDMC misspelled as "limdmct" so the keyword was silently ignored
    * Misleading NUMBA_NUM_THREADS advice in parallel.rst: JIT kernels are single-threaded
    * Cusp initialisation description in cusp.rst: Cusp is created by CuspFactory,
      not by Slater
    * Missing underscores in DMC_EQUIL_NBLOCK and DMC_STATS_NBLOCK keyword names in config.rst
    * Third derivatives of Slater orbitals: yyy and zzz components of tressian_matrix
      used the x-component of the angular gradient, and tressian doubled one ordering
      of the triple-product trace instead of summing both (verified against numerical
      derivatives on Ne)
    * False trace-reversal identity in tressian snippets of slater.rst
    * Broken Numba documentation link in index.rst
    * Incorrect output description for kinetic_energy_parameters_d1 in wfn.rst
    * Missing backflow-parameters formula in kinetic_energy_parameters_d1 section of wfn.rst
    * Incorrect output description for nonlocal_energy_parameters_d1 and missing sign
      in kinetic energy derivative in wfn.rst
    * Methods summary in wfn.rst attributed to Slater class instead of Wfn
    * Duplicate Sphinx anchor ``intermediate data`` between jastrow.rst and backflow.rst
    * Duplicate Sphinx anchor ``value`` between wfn.rst and slater.rst
    * Typo "Input parameter are supported" → "Input parameters supported" in config.rst
    * emin linear method took Q = 1 / eigvector[0]**2 - 1, which holds only for an eigvector
      normalized in the S metric, while scipy.linalg.eig returns a Euclidean-normalized one,
      so the semi-orthogonalization rescaling of Δp was wrong whenever the derivatives were
      strongly correlated; Q is now evaluated as Δp · S · Δp
    * emin linear method divided by the standard deviation of every wave function derivative,
      so a parameter the sample does not depend on produced an infinite scale and a singular
      overlap matrix; such parameters are now excluded from the eigenproblem and left unchanged
    * UNR drift-velocity limiting used the squared drift of the whole configuration for every
      electron instead of the electron's own, over-limiting the step by a factor that grows with
      the electron count. On Be the drift was 0.7% shorter on average and up to 8% in the tail.
      The limiting factor is also evaluated as 2 / (1 + sqrt(1 + 2*a*v^2*t)), which is the same
      expression without the cancellation at small v
    * CBCS branching used the UNR reweighting factor (23), which applies
      exp[t_eff*(S(r) + S(r'))/2] to an accepted move and exp[t_eff*S(r)] to a rejected one,
      rather than (25), which applies exp[t_eff*((1 - p/2)*S(r) + p/2*S(r'))] to every move and
      is what Casino uses. The two agree as t -> 0 and differ in the time-step error, which is
      what UNR report and what a fixed-node energy at finite t sees: the gap grows with the
      spread of the local energy over a move, so it is invisible for He, where the trial function
      is nearly exact, and largest for Be with backflow. The local energy is now evaluated at the
      proposed position even when the move is rejected, which costs one extra evaluation on the
      8% of moves that are rejected. EBES keeps (23), as Casino does, since there is no single
      acceptance probability for the configuration
    * The effective time step was t * <p> instead of the UNR (24) estimator
      t * <p*|dr|^2> / <|dr|^2>, which is not the same average whenever the acceptance is
      correlated with the size of the diffusive displacement, as it is. The diffusive
      displacement is now measured from the drifted position in the near-nucleus branch of the
      modified Green's function too, where the sampled offset from the nucleus was used
    * The energy jacobian and hessian were divided by the number of parameters rather than the
      number of configurations. Both carry the same factor, so the Newton step itself was
      unaffected and only the gradient the optimizer reports and the thresholds it converges
      against saw the wrong scale
    * emin centered the energy gradient over the sample. That changes nothing in the i,j block of
      H, where wfn_gradient is centered already, and only drops <E_L,j> from the first row,
      turning the exact g_L into g_R; that term is what keeps delta p exact on a finite sample
      when psi_lin is an eigenstate
* Improved
    * CBCS initial time step is now sqrt(3) * erfinv(1/2) * (1 + 0.045/nuclei) / sqrt(<T>) instead
      of one over the electron count, with <T> summed over the nuclei from the Thomas-Fermi
      expansion 0.7687*Z^(7/3) - 0.5*Z^2 + 0.2699*Z^(5/3) and
      nuclei = sum(Z^(7/3))^2 / sum(Z^(14/3)). Only the last constant is fitted. A uniform
      displacement by ts gives Var(ln(psi'^2/psi^2)) = 8/3 * ts^2 * <T> while detailed balance
      pins the mean of that log ratio to minus half its variance, so a gaussian one is accepted
      with probability 2*Phi(-sigma/2) and the 50% point sits at ts*sqrt(<T>) = sqrt(3)*erfinv(1/2)
      = 0.826; <T> = abs(E) follows from the virial theorem, and the Thomas-Fermi expansion reproduces
      the Hartree-Fock energies to 1.8% with no free constant. The remaining correction is the
      non-gaussianity of the log ratio, which falls off with the number of nuclei sharing <T>,
      since the drift is of order Z at a nucleus and outer shells contribute almost nothing
      to the tails.
      Measured on the seventeen systems in examples/time_step/CBCS the 50% acceptance point lands
      within 0.9% rms of the initial guess for atoms, ions, hydrides and hydrocarbons alike, where
      the old guess spread it over 0.61 to 1.86 and left molecules against the edge of the scanned
      range. The electron count deliberately does not appear: it equals sum(Z) only for a neutral
      system, and substituting it costs 27% on Be(2+)
    * optimize_vmc_step no longer scans eleven step sizes and fits them to an empirical
      two-parameter curve. Since sigma is proportional to the step size, one measurement fixes the
      whole curve: inverting the gaussian acceptance law at the measured A and reading it back at
      1/2 gives ts <- ts * Phi^-1(3/4) / Phi^-1(1 - A/2), whose fixed point is A = 1/2 for any monotone
      acceptance curve, so the law sets only the rate of convergence and the map cannot converge to
      the wrong step size where the gaussian fails. Replayed on the curves in
      examples/time_step/CBCS it takes a 40% error to 2%, then 0.2%, then 0.02%. Three iterations
      are used, starting from the current step size rather than resetting to the initial guess, so
      a re-optimization after a wave-function update is a single correction, and the curve_fit
      failure path is gone
    * vmc_step_graph now places its points equally in acceptance rather than in step size, nineteen
      targets from 0.95 to 0.05 through the same inverse, anchored on the 50% point the optimizer
      has just measured. A grid in step size spends most of its points in the saturated tails, where
      the acceptance says nothing about the 50% point, and spends them at system-dependent places.
      Step sizes are still written out in atomic units, and each point also reports the kinetic
      energy the law implies there, 3/(8*ts^2) * (2*Phi^-1(1 - A/2))^2, which is flat across the
      grid exactly when the law holds. Its level is fixed by <T>, which the same run now measures
      on the same walk and writes into the header, so each file is self-contained and the column
      divided by that value is comparable between systems
    * Initial time step for pseudopotentials: Thomas-Fermi is a semiclassical theory of many
      electrons and overshoots fourfold on pseudo-carbon, 13.2 against a measured 3.3, by placing
      the four valence electrons in a 1s shell. Those are hydrogenic instead,
      Z_v*(0.65*Z_v + 0.35)^2 / (2*n^2) per pseudoatom with the principal number of the row and
      Slater screening by the other valence electrons: exact on hydrogen and 10 to 50% high across
      the second row. The virial theorem does not survive a pseudopotential either, <T>/abs(E_val)
      running from 0.62 on carbon to 0.81 on neon, but the sum rule itself does: over the 184
      all-electron and 72 pseudopotential CBCS runs under examples, dtvmc*KEI is constant at
      0.261 and 0.293 against a gaussian 0.227475, over a factor of 1160 in <T>
    * The nuclei participation ratio is now taken over the kinetic energy shares themselves,
      sum(T_a)^2 / sum(T_a^2), which is the same number to 0.03% for all-electron systems and
      extends to pseudoatoms
    * EBES and DBDS initial time steps now follow from their own sum rule, N and 2 times the CBCS
      dtvmc (sqrt(N) and sqrt(2) in the displacement), instead of 1/log(max(Z)) and 1/N. Both are
      exact statements about Var(X); what the EBES factor is knowingly missing is the spread of
      grad_i(ln psi) between electrons, which the sum rule averages over, so the guess is off by
      1.0 to 2.6 on anything with more than one occupied shell; optimize_vmc_step supplies the
      rest, its fixed point not depending on the model
    * VMC proposes from a gaussian rather than a cube, in CBCS and EBES alike, and dtvmc is carried
      as the variance of one displacement component exactly as Casino states it, so the two codes'
      step sizes are directly comparable and no sqrt(3) conversion is left anywhere. The reason is
      EBES: X = ln(psi'^2/psi^2) is a sum of three terms there, one electron by three axes, so no
      central limit theorem is available and a cube leaves X an Irwin-Hall variable - the excess
      kurtosis of -0.33 measured on one-shell systems is the proposal and not the wave function.
      With a gaussian displacement 2*dr*grad(ln psi) is exactly gaussian at leading order however
      few components move. The sum rule reads Var(X) = 8*dtvmc*<T>, the 50% point sits at
      dtvmc*<T> = erfinv(1/2)^2 = 0.227475, and the initial guess, the acceptance inversion and
      both diagnostic graphs are stated in those units. Existing dtvmc values keep their meaning.
      Re-measured over all 31 systems in examples/time_step, both modes: with the cube removed
      the width of X at its measured variance comes out at 1.000 on every one-shell system,
      He 0.9995 and Li+ 1.0013 in CBCS, He 1.0008 and Li+ 1.0040 in EBES, where the cube gave
      0.956 and 0.918 - the deficit doubling in EBES is the missing central limit theorem, and
      the cube's excess kurtosis reaches -0.719 on pseudo-hydrogen at small step, which is
      -1.2*E[sum n^4] = -0.72 for three components with an isotropic drift. It also drifts to
      zero along the acceptance curve, so no fitted constant could have absorbed it
    * The fitted correction to the initial CBCS step is now 1 + 0.080/nuclei^0.82 instead of
      1 + 0.045/nuclei, refitted on the gaussian campaign: 0.3% rms and 0.8% at worst over the
      seventeen all-electron systems, against 0.9% rms before. The old constant was hiding the
      shape of the cube, which moved the 50% point by 4% in a system-dependent way and left a
      spurious Z dependence inside it - over the atoms and ions the fitted value ran from 0.038
      on Be to 0.065 on Kr, where the gaussian holds 0.075 to 0.086. Gaussian and Slater twins
      of the same atom now agree to 0.3%
    * Slater value, gradient and laplacian are built in one pass over the basis, so the local
      energy makes one pass where it made two. A solid harmonic is homogeneous of degree l and
      harmonic, so r•∇Y = l•Y and ΔY = 0, which leaves the laplacian of an orbital as
      Y * (r**2 * radial_1 + (2*l + 3) * radial_2), out of the same radial sums the gradient is
      made of: neither the exponentials nor the angular parts are evaluated twice, and one inverse
      of the slater matrix serves both traces. A Slater-only VMC run is now as fast as Casino from
      about ten electrons and faster beyond that, see examples/stowfn/READ.ME
    * Statistics are accumulated as the run goes and the local energies of the whole run are no
      longer held anywhere. Reblocking keeps one partial sum per block length, so its storage grows
      with the logarithm of the number of steps, and the block length is the first one satisfying
      the Wolff criterion 8**k >= 2 * nstep * ncorr**2. The correlation time is integrated over the
      autocorrelation function with Sokal's self-consistent window w >= 3*tau, measured on the block
      it belongs to, instead of summing the partial autocorrelations of a Burg AR fit over the whole
      run: on an AR(1) chain of a known tau of 4 it gives 3.964 +- 0.063 where Burg gave 4.776.
      Block averages, variances and their errors are accumulated per block, which retires the
      shared MPI window that held every local energy of the run, and the walk is taken in chunks
      bounded to 10 MB of positions, the tail a subsequent DMC run starts from being carried across
      the chunk boundaries. The memory of a VMC run is now flat in vmc_nstep and vmc_nblock: O3 at
      vmc_nstep 1e8 on four processes went from 12.3 GB per node to 0.24 GB. The energy, its
      uncorrected error and the reblocked error are unchanged to twelve digits
    * VMC block header reports the acceptance ratio, the correlation time with its error, and the
      efficiency nmove / (time * variance * tau), the wall time an independent sample of a given
      variance costs, which is the quantity vmc_decorr_period is there to minimize
    * pyblock, pandas and statsmodels are no longer dependencies
    * Speed up backflow optimization: kinetic_energy_parameters_d1 uses slater.tressian_dot
      to avoid materializing the (3N)³ tressian tensor, vectorized bf_d1 loop and
      fused reduction loop in backflow_laplacian_parameters_d1
    * Cusp correction page expanded with physical motivation, correction scheme, and API reference

v 0.3.0 (2025-10-30)
--------------------
* Added
    * Documentation on https://casinoqmc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/
* Improved
    * Improve Jastrow speed by 20%
    * scipy.optimize.least_squares optimization in one thread
    * Add tests
* Fixes
    * Remove ``np.float_`` as it not supported in Numpy 2.X
    * Fix reweighted VMC variance optimization.

v 0.2.0 (2024-10-30)
--------------------
* Added
    * Pseudopotential for VMC and DMC (with T-move)
* Improved
    * Use Horner Scheme in Jastrow term
    * Electron-by-Electron (EBES) sampling in DMC
    * Use structrefs instead of jitclasses
* Fixes
    * System with only up- or only down- electrons (i.e H-atom)

v 0.1.0 (2023-10-30)
--------------------
* Added
    * Orbital file formats: gwfn.data, stowfn.data
    * Multi-determinant expansions
    * 3-term Jastrow factor and 3-term backflow transformation
    * Configuration-by-configuration (CBCS) sampling
    * Partial cusp correction according to A. Ma, M. D. Towler, N. D. Drummond, and R. J. Needs, without rc optimization
    * MPI parallelization
    * VMC and DMC energy calculation, varmin and emin optimization
